## Additional OpenVPN 2.7 Accounting Work

This branch contains the standalone `Framed-Route` handling fix.

A more complete branch is also available:

`fix/openvpn-27-accounting`

That branch includes this `Framed-Route` improvement together with restored and extended RADIUS accounting support for modern OpenVPN 2.7 installations.

It adds:

- RADIUS Accounting-Start
- RADIUS Accounting-Stop
- Acct-Session-Time
- Input/output byte accounting
- RADIUS Gigawords support
- Acct-Terminate-Cause
- User-Request
- Lost-Carrier
- Idle-Timeout
- Session-Timeout
- OpenVPN CLIENT_CONNECT support
- OpenVPN CLIENT_DISCONNECT support
- OpenVPN termination reason monitor
- systemd service for termination reason detection
- the same improved Framed-Route handling contained in this branch

For users who need both improved route handling and RADIUS accounting, use:

`fix/openvpn-27-accounting`

The `fix/framed-route-push` branch is intentionally kept separate as a minimal Framed-Route-only change.

# Slon4e fork — improved Framed-Route handling for OpenVPN remote-access clients

This fork contains an improvement to the handling of the RADIUS `Framed-Route` attribute in `openvpn-auth-radius`, primarily intended for OpenVPN remote-access deployments using daloRADIUS as the frontend for FreeRADIUS.

The tested configuration flow is:

daloRADIUS → FreeRADIUS → `Framed-Route` → `openvpn-auth-radius` → OpenVPN CCD → `push "route ..."`

The original `openvpn-auth-radius` implementation writes RADIUS `Framed-Route` attributes to the OpenVPN client configuration directory as `iroute` directives.

For example:

```text
Framed-Route = 10.10.0.0/16
```

was written as:

```text
iroute 10.10.0.0 255.255.0.0
```

`iroute` is appropriate when the specified network is located behind an OpenVPN client.

For standard remote-access VPN users, however, the desired behavior is often to push the route to the connecting client.

This fork therefore changes the generated CCD directive to:

```text
push "route 10.10.0.0 255.255.0.0"
```

instead of:

```text
iroute 10.10.0.0 255.255.0.0
```

The `Framed-Route` parser has also been simplified.

The original implementation expects the traditional RADIUS route format:

```text
10.10.0.0/16 10.8.0.1/32 1
```

where the additional values represent the gateway and metric.

For routes pushed to OpenVPN remote-access clients, these values are not required. The updated implementation therefore accepts a simple network/CIDR value:

```text
10.10.0.0/16
```

Gateway and metric fields are no longer required for this use case. If they are supplied, values following the `network/prefix` portion are ignored by the client-pushed-route implementation.

This is particularly useful when different OpenVPN server instances use different tunnel networks.

Example:

```text
TCP OpenVPN instance: 10.8.0.0/24
UDP OpenVPN instance: 10.9.0.0/24
```

FreeRADIUS can return the same route attributes for users connecting through either instance:

```text
Framed-Route = 10.10.0.0/16
Framed-Route = 10.30.30.0/24
```

There is no requirement to configure a TCP-specific gateway such as `10.8.0.1` or a UDP-specific gateway such as `10.9.0.1` in the `Framed-Route` value.

When daloRADIUS is used as the frontend for FreeRADIUS, the routes can therefore be managed directly as per-user Reply Attributes:

```text
Framed-Route += 10.10.0.0/16
Framed-Route += 10.30.30.0/24
```

FreeRADIUS returns these attributes in the Access-Accept response and `openvpn-auth-radius` generates the corresponding OpenVPN CCD configuration:

```text
push "route 10.10.0.0 255.255.0.0"
push "route 10.30.30.0 255.255.255.0"
```

This removes the need to manually maintain per-user pushed routes in OpenVPN CCD files.

## CIDR-to-netmask fix

The original CIDR-to-netmask conversion code could generate malformed IPv4 netmasks for some prefixes.

For example:

```text
/16 -> 255.255.0
```

instead of:

```text
/16 -> 255.255.0.0
```

This fork replaces the old conversion logic with direct 32-bit IPv4 netmask generation.

Examples:

```text
/8  -> 255.0.0.0
/16 -> 255.255.0.0
/20 -> 255.255.240.0
/24 -> 255.255.255.0
/30 -> 255.255.255.252
/32 -> 255.255.255.255
```

## Example with daloRADIUS / FreeRADIUS

Configure the user's Reply Attributes in daloRADIUS as:

```text
Framed-Route += 10.10.0.0/16
Framed-Route += 10.30.30.0/24
```

FreeRADIUS returns:

```text
Framed-Route = 10.10.0.0/16
Framed-Route = 10.30.30.0/24
```

and the plugin writes:

```text
push "route 10.10.0.0 255.255.0.0"
push "route 10.30.30.0 255.255.255.0"
```

## Tested environment

The changes in this fork have been successfully tested with:

```text
Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS
OpenVPN 2.7.x
FreeRADIUS 3.x
daloRADIUS
openvpn-auth-radius
```

The test environment used separate TCP and UDP OpenVPN server instances with different VPN subnets and per-user route definitions managed through daloRADIUS / FreeRADIUS.

## Building this fork

On Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS:

```bash
apt update
apt install -y git build-essential libgcrypt20-dev

git clone https://github.com/Slon4e/openvpn-auth-radius.git
cd openvpn-auth-radius

make
```

The resulting plugin is:

```text
radiusplugin.so
```

It may be installed separately from the distribution-provided version:

```bash
mkdir -p /usr/local/lib/openvpn
cp radiusplugin.so /usr/local/lib/openvpn/radiusplugin-custom.so
chmod 755 /usr/local/lib/openvpn/radiusplugin-custom.so
```

Example OpenVPN configuration:

```text
plugin /usr/local/lib/openvpn/radiusplugin-custom.so /etc/openvpn/radiusplugin-tcp.cnf
```

or for a UDP OpenVPN instance:

```text
plugin /usr/local/lib/openvpn/radiusplugin-custom.so /etc/openvpn/radiusplugin-udp.cnf
```

## Compatibility note

This change intentionally changes the interpretation of `Framed-Route` for this fork from an OpenVPN `iroute` to a client-pushed `route`.

If your deployment uses `Framed-Route` to describe networks that are located behind an OpenVPN client, the original upstream `iroute` behavior may be more appropriate.

